] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Competition dates"] "\n" [:td {} "\n14 May – 28 October\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "OCOG"] "\n" [:td {} [:a {:href "/organizations/363"} "Concours d'exercices physiques et de sports (1900)"]] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Participants"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/counts/edition/2"} "1241"] "\nfrom " [:a {:href "/editions/2/countries"} "26 countries"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Medal events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n95\nin 22 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:th {} "Other events"] "\n" [:td {} "\n266\nin 23 disciplines\n"] "\n"] "\n"]] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "\nOverview\n\n"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "The 1900 and " [:a {:href "/editions/3"} "1904 Olympics"] " were associated with world fairs and became Games quite unlike anything we know today.  In 1900, Paris hosted a great world’s fair, the Exposition Universelle Internationale de 1900 à Paris. " [:a {:href "/athletes/920613"} "Coubertin"] " made plans to hold the Olympics as part of the fair and planned to organize the events, but the organizers of the fair relegated Coubertin to a relatively minor administrative position and took over the organization of the sporting events connected with the fair."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Most of the events we today consider “Olympic” were not even labeled as such in 1900, often being called the “Championnats d’Exposition”. Years later, many athletes did not know they had competed in the Olympic Games, believing that their sport had been only a part of the world’s fair. Only the " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/ATH"} "athletics (track &amp; field) events"] " were really publicized in the media as being part of the Olympics."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The Games were stretched out over five months (May-October), and true Opening and Closing Ceremonies were not held. It is difficult to know, because of the confusion over titles, and the many, many events held at the fair, what events should actually be considered “Olympic” and which should not. The IOC had no real control of this and, thus, one sees various listings. Many unusual sports and events were contested, such as " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/PBT"} "motorboating"] ", " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/BLN"} "balloon racing"] ", " [:a {:href "/results/4432"} "underwater swimming"] ", and an " [:a {:href "/results/4433"} "obstacle swimming race"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The athletics caused a great deal of controversy. The French scheduled Sunday competition, which was not considered " [:em {} "de rigeur"] " in the United States in 1900. The Americans threatened not to compete, and the French at first reconsidered, moving many of the events to 14 July. However, 14 July was Bastille Day, so the French moved the start of some events back to Sunday, 15 July. The Americans were confused by much of this, and many of them, notably several pole vaulters, failed to show for their events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The star of the Games was " [:a {:href "/athletes/78667"} "Al Kraenzlein"] " who, through 2024, remains the only athlete to win four individual gold medals in track &amp; field at a single Olympics. In the " [:a {:href "/results/56096"} "long jump"] ", Kraenzlein met his arch-rival, " [:a {:href "/athletes/78917"} "Meyer Prinstein"] ", a Jew. Both competed on Saturday, with Prinstein leading the qualifying for the long jump. The finals were to be held on Sunday and Prinstein refused to compete out of principle, although he had competed on his Sabbath. Kraenzlein did compete on Sunday and posted a winning jump that surpassed Prinstein’s Saturday effort. Prinstein proposed a Monday jump-off, which Kraenzlein refused. Prinstein then punched Kraenzlein in the mouth and the two feuded for years."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Women made their Olympic début, with the first known competitors being croquet players and one yachtswoman, " [:a {:href "/athletes/61789"} "Hélène de Pourtalès"] ". " [:a {:href "/athletes/45"} "Charlotte Cooper"] " (GBR) won the first championships by a woman, in " [:a {:href "/results/44057"} "tennis singles"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/44067"} "mixed doubles"] ". " [:a {:href "/athletes/18138"} "Margaret Abbott"] " (USA) won the " [:a {:href "/results/40001"} "“Olympic” golf championship"] " in early October. Neither of those two sports were labeled as Olympic by the organizers. Years later, Abbott’s relatives did not know for certain that the title she won that day had been for the Olympic championship. Incidentally, in some sports, medals were not awarded. Most of the listed prizes were cups and other similar trophies. In several sports, notably " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/FEN"} "fencing"] " and " [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/SHO"} "shooting"] ", professional events were held and considered later by the IOC to be “Olympic” events."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Of the 1900 Olympics, the best that can be said is that there was very little of true Olympic stature. The Olympic Movement awaited better, but it would have to wait for eight years."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Bid process"] "\n" [:div {:class "description"} "\n" [:p {} "Paris was selected to host the 1900 Olympic Games at the " [:a {:href "/meetings"} "Sorbonne Congress"] " in Paris, on 24 June 1894, after Athina had been selected to host the 1896 Olympic Games."] "\n\n"] "\n\n" [:h2 {} "Medal Disciplines"] "\n" [:table {:class "table table-striped"} "\n" [:tbody {} [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/ARC"} "Archery"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/EDV"} "Equestrian Driving"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/SAL"} "Sailing"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/GAR"} "Artistic Gymnastics"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/EJP"} "Equestrian Jumping"] "\n"] "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/SHO"} "Shooting"] "\n"] "\n"] "\n" [:tr {} "\n" [:td {} "\n" [:a {:href "/editions/2/sports/ATH"} "Athletics"] "\n"